      </div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
        Your First Day in the Park — What It Actually Feels Like
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-8 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-stone-800">6:45 AM.</strong> You walk from your hotel in Wulingyuan town — past the breakfast stalls selling steamed buns and soy milk — to the park gate. There is already a short queue of domestic tourists with selfie sticks and hiking poles. You hand over your passport at the ticket window, they scan it, and you are through in two minutes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-stone-800">The shuttle bus takes you to the Bailong Elevator.</strong> The ride is 15 minutes through dense forest, the road climbing steadily. You get off and walk up to the elevator complex — a glass-and-steel structure built directly into the cliff face, 326 meters tall. The queue is maybe 8 minutes at 7:30 AM. You step into the glass-walled car with 20 other people, the doors close, and <em>then</em> — the forest canopy drops away and the pillars appear, one after another, as the elevator rises through open air. People gasp. It is genuinely thrilling, even on your fifth time.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-stone-800">At the top, you walk 5 minutes to the Avatar Hallelujah Mountain viewpoint.</strong> If you timed it right — before 8:30 AM — the mist is still hanging in the valley below the pillar. It looks exactly like it is floating. You take your photos, then walk 200 meters further along the trail to the unmarked second viewpoint where there is nobody. Same pillar, same angle, zero crowd. This is the moment you realize the guidebooks were not exaggerating.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-stone-800">By 10 AM the tour groups have arrived.</strong> The main platforms are busy but not unbearable — you have already seen the best stuff. You take the shuttle to Tianzi Mountain, cable car up (CNY 72), and eat a bowl of noodles at the summit food court while looking at a sea of pillars stretching to the horizon. By 2 PM you are walking Golden Whip Stream in the golden afternoon light, monkeys in the trees overhead, the sound of water beside you. You exit the park around 4:30 PM, legs tired, camera full, and you understand why people come back.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-stone-400 border-l-2 border-forest-300 pl-4">
        <span class="font-semibold text-stone-500">This is not a hypothetical.</span> We have done this exact route on four separate trips — it works. Start early, hit the Avatar pillar before 9 AM, and the rest of the day flows naturally. See our <a href="/itinerary/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">detailed 1-day itinerary</a> for the step-by-step schedule with exact times and costs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

