      <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl divide-y divide-stone-200">
        {[
          { q: "How much do tickets actually cost?", a: "Park entry is CNY 225 (Mar-Nov) or CNY 115 (Dec-Feb) for a 4-day pass. Cable cars and the Bailong Elevator cost extra: budget CNY 350-450 for in-park transport over 2-3 days. A full visit with meals and accommodation runs CNY 500-800 per person per day. Students and seniors (60+) get 50% off. Children under 1.2m and seniors 70+ enter free. See our <a href='/tickets/' class='text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline'>full ticket breakdown</a>." },
          { q: "When is the best time to visit?", a: "April and October-November are ideal. April has wildflowers and morning mist. Late October through November has autumn colors, clear skies, and half the summer crowds. Avoid May 1-5 (Labor Day) and October 1-7 (Golden Week) — the park hits capacity and queues stretch for hours. Winter (Dec-Feb) is cold but uncrowded, with half-price entry and occasional snow on the pillars. See <a href='#when-to-go' class='text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline'>our season-by-season guide above</a>." },
          { q: "How do I get to Zhangjiajie?", a: "Fly into Zhangjiajie Hehua Airport (DYG) — direct flights from Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, Chengdu. Or fly to Changsha and take the high-speed train (2h45m, ~CNY 150) — this often saves CNY 500-800. From Zhangjiajie city, the Wulingyuan park entrance is a 40-minute bus (CNY 12) or taxi (CNY 80-120). See <a href='#at-a-glance' class='text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline'>our Getting There guide above</a>." },
          { q: "Can I see the Avatar mountains without hiking?", a: "Yes. The Bailong Elevator (world's tallest outdoor lift, 326m) takes you directly to Yuanjiajie where the Avatar Hallelujah Mountain is a 5-minute walk from the exit. Cable cars, elevators, and shuttle buses cover most major viewpoints. Golden Whip Stream is a flat, paved 7.5 km walk suitable for all fitness levels. Tianmen Mountain's 999-step staircase to Heaven's Gate can be bypassed with an escalator (CNY 32) built inside the mountain." },
          { q: "Is English widely spoken?", a: "Limited English at major hotels and park entrances. Most park signs are bilingual (Chinese/English). Download Google Translate's offline Chinese pack before you go. Save key phrases on your phone. Outside the park, especially in local restaurants and on buses, English is rare — pointing at menu photos and using translation apps is how we navigate." },
          { q: "How many days do I need?", a: "3 full days is the sweet spot: Day 1 Forest Park core (Yuanjiajie + Tianzi), Day 2 Tianmen Mountain, Day 3 Grand Canyon + Glass Bridge. 5 days adds Fenghuang Ancient Town, Yangjiajie hiking, and Baofeng Lake at a relaxed pace. 1 day works for a stopover — hit the Avatar pillar, Tianzi Mountain, and Golden Whip Stream, but skip Tianmen and the Glass Bridge. See our <a href='/itinerary/' class='text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline'>itinerary plans</a>." },
          { q: "Where should I stay?", a: "Wulingyuan town — right at the park gate, 5-15 minute walk to the entrance. Hundreds of hotels from CNY 150-800/night. This is where we stay on every trip. Zhangjiajie city center is cheaper (CNY 80-500) but adds 40 minutes of commuting each way. A few basic guesthouses operate inside the park (CNY 200-400) — spartan but you wake up to mist-filled valleys with zero tourists. See our <a href='/hotels/' class='text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline'>accommodation guide</a>." },
          { q: "Is Zhangjiajie safe for solo travelers?", a: "Yes. The park is well-patrolled with paved trails, guardrails, and clear signage. Violent crime is extremely rare. The main risks are practical: slippery steps after rain, dehydration on summer hikes, and getting lost on unmarked Yangjiajie trails (download offline maps). As a solo traveler you will have no issues. The biggest challenge is the language barrier, not safety." },
                    { q: "Do I need a visa for China?", a: "Most foreign visitors need a Chinese tourist visa (L visa) before arrival. Apply at your nearest Chinese embassy or visa center — processing takes 4-7 business days. The 144-hour (6-day) transit visa is available through certain cities including Changsha, but it restricts you to Hunan province. Check your country's specific requirements on the Chinese embassy website. Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned departure date." },
                    { q: "Can I visit as a day trip from Changsha?", a: "Yes, but it is a long day. The high-speed train from Changsha to Zhangjiajie West takes 2h45m (~CNY 150). Leave Changsha before 7 AM, arrive at the park by 10:30 AM, and you have 6-7 hours before catching the last train back around 8 PM. You can see Yuanjiajie (Avatar pillar) and Golden Whip Stream in one push. Skip Tianmen Mountain and the Glass Bridge — they are too far for a day trip. For the best experience, stay at least one night in Wulingyuan." },
